if (ret < 0)
    goto out_unlock;
...
idrobj = idr_replace(&file_priv->object_idr, obj, handle);
WARN_ON(idrobj != NULL);

Reason:
The early exit check `if (ret < 0) goto out_unlock;` was placed after the successful `idr_alloc` but before the normal replacement of the new handle, causing the IDR to remain NULL for the new handle in the normal flow. Additionally, the `idr_replace` that should have been executed in the normal path was incorrectly placed inside the error path of `drm_prime_add_buf_handle`, so it was only called when adding the dma_buf handle failed. This resulted in the new handle pointer never being replaced from NULL to the actual gem object, leading to an inconsistent handle-to-object mapping and triggering a warning in `drm_prime_destroy_file_private`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
idr_remove(&file_priv->object_idr, args->handle);
idrobj = idr_replace(&file_priv->object_idr, obj, handle);
WARN_ON(idrobj != NULL);

Reason:
The fix removes the erroneous early exit check and the misplaced `idr_replace` from the error path. Instead, after successfully removing the old handle from the IDR, the new handle's entry (which was previously allocated as NULL) is replaced with the actual gem object pointer. This ensures that the IDR mapping is correctly updated in the normal execution flow, preventing the inconsistency that triggered the warning.

